-- Migration: Question generation — AUD batch 6 (30 questions)
-- Date: 2026-02-23
-- Topic: Misstatements and Control Deficiencies
-- Difficulty: 9E / 15M / 6H
-- Bloom's: L1:8 / L2:9 / L3:8 / L4:5

BEGIN;

INSERT INTO questions (section_id, topic, stem, choices, correct_index, explanation, difficulty, cognitive_level)
VALUES

-- Q1: medium / L2 — Clearly trivial misstatement threshold
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Birchwood Electronics, the engagement team identifies a $175 misstatement in office supplies expense. Overall materiality is $250,000. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should treat this misstatement as:', '["Clearly trivial and not required to be accumulated on the summary of uncorrected misstatements", "Immaterial but required to be accumulated for aggregate evaluation", "Material and requiring immediate correction by management before issuing the report", "A projected misstatement requiring extrapolation to the full population"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.05, misstatements are clearly trivial when they are inconsequential both individually and in the aggregate, typically well below 5% of materiality. At $175 against $250,000 materiality, this amount is clearly trivial and need not be accumulated. While choice B reflects proper treatment for misstatements above the clearly trivial threshold, AU-C 450 explicitly exempts clearly trivial items from the accumulation requirement.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q2: hard / L3 — IT general control deficiency affecting multiple FS areas
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Granite Peak Financial Services relies on an ERP system for all accounting functions. The engagement team discovers that the IT department failed to implement access controls over program change management for three months during the fiscal year, affecting automated controls over revenue, payroll, and accounts payable. Under AU-C 265, how should the auditor analyze the severity of this IT general control deficiency?', '["Evaluate each affected automated control independently without considering the IT deficiency''s pervasive nature", "Limit the evaluation to the financial statement area with the largest dollar exposure to the deficiency", "Consider the combined magnitude of potential misstatements across all financial statement areas affected by the compromised IT controls", "Classify the deficiency automatically as a significant deficiency because it involves information technology systems"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A11, when an IT general control deficiency affects multiple automated application controls, the auditor must consider the combined effect across all affected financial statement areas because the deficiency is pervasive. The potential magnitude encompasses all processes relying on the compromised IT controls. Choice A is incorrect because isolating each control ignores the compounding effect that a single IT deficiency has on downstream automated controls.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L2 — Deficiency in design vs deficiency in operation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Clearwater Manufacturing has a documented policy requiring the controller to review and approve all journal entries exceeding $10,000. During the audit, the engagement team discovers that the controller approved all sampled entries but did not actually examine the supporting documentation before signing. Under AU-C 265, this situation represents:', '["A deficiency in design because the control policy is inadequate for its objective", "A material weakness because the control failed to operate throughout the period", "A compensating control failure requiring expanded substantive testing of all journal entries", "A deficiency in operation because the control exists but is not operating effectively as designed"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A1, a deficiency in operation exists when a properly designed control does not operate as designed or when the person performing the control lacks the authority or competence to perform it effectively. Clearwater''s policy is appropriately designed, but the controller is not executing the review as intended. Choice A is incorrect because the control''s design—requiring review and approval of large journal entries—is adequate; the failure is in execution, not design.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Fraud-related misstatement effect on control deficiency evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Ridgemont Distributors, the auditor discovers that a warehouse manager manipulated inventory counts to conceal a $35,000 theft. Overall materiality is $500,000. Under AU-C 265, how does the fraud-related nature of this misstatement affect the auditor''s evaluation of the related control deficiency?', '["The fraud-related nature has no bearing on severity because the misstatement is below materiality", "The presence of fraud elevates the control deficiency to at least a significant deficiency regardless of the dollar amount involved", "The control deficiency is automatically classified as a material weakness whenever fraud is detected", "The auditor should reduce the severity assessment because the fraud was detected during the audit"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A14, indicators of a material weakness include fraud by senior management, and fraud of any amount is a strong indicator that a significant deficiency or material weakness exists. Even though $35,000 is below materiality, the presence of fraud signals that controls failed to prevent or detect unauthorized transactions. Choice C overstates the requirement—while fraud often results in a material weakness, the auditor must still evaluate severity based on the facts and circumstances.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Management representation about uncorrected misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'At the conclusion of the audit of Evergreen Valley Hospital, the auditor has accumulated $78,000 in uncorrected misstatements. Management has reviewed the list and decided not to correct these items. Under AU-C 450, what specific representation should the auditor obtain from management?', '["A representation that management will correct all misstatements in the subsequent period", "A representation confirming that the auditor approved management''s decision not to correct the misstatements", "A representation acknowledging that management has no responsibility for the accumulated uncorrected misstatements", "A representation that the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 450.14, the auditor must request a written representation from management that the effects of uncorrected misstatements are immaterial, both individually and in the aggregate, to the financial statements as a whole. The summary of uncorrected misstatements should be included with or attached to this representation. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 450 does not require management to promise future corrections—the representation addresses the current-period materiality assessment.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: medium / L2 — Summary of uncorrected misstatements documentation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Maple Ridge Construction''s audit team has accumulated seven uncorrected misstatements totaling $92,000, with overall materiality set at $200,000. The engagement partner requests documentation of the misstatement evaluation. Under AU-C 450, the auditor''s documentation of uncorrected misstatements must include:', '["Only those misstatements that individually exceed 50% of overall materiality", "Only projected misstatements identified through sampling procedures", "The amount below which misstatements are regarded as clearly trivial, and all accumulated misstatements above that threshold", "A listing of only the three largest misstatements identified during the audit"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.15, the auditor must document the clearly trivial threshold, all misstatements accumulated during the audit above that threshold, and the conclusion on whether uncorrected misstatements are material. This ensures the audit file demonstrates a thorough evaluation process. Choice B is incorrect because AU-C 450 requires documentation of all accumulated misstatements—factual, judgmental, and projected—not only those arising from sampling extrapolations.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q7: medium / L3 — Prior-year deficiency remains uncorrected
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the prior-year audit of Pinnacle Aerospace, the auditor communicated a significant deficiency in the revenue recognition process to the audit committee. During the current-year audit, the auditor determines that management has not remediated the deficiency. Under AU-C 265, what is the effect of a previously communicated significant deficiency that remains uncorrected?', '["The auditor is not required to communicate it again because the audit committee was already informed", "The auditor must automatically upgrade the classification to a material weakness due to recurring status", "The auditor should communicate only verbally to the audit committee as a courtesy reminder", "The auditor must communicate the deficiency again in the current year''s written communication"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.13, the auditor must communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses identified during the current audit in writing, even if they were previously communicated. A recurring deficiency that management has not remediated remains a current-year finding. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 265 does not exempt previously reported deficiencies—each year''s communication must address all significant deficiencies and material weaknesses identified in the current audit.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q8: easy / L1 — Clearly trivial definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, what does the term ''clearly trivial'' mean when applied to audit misstatements?', '["Misstatements that are inconsequential both individually and in the aggregate", "Misstatements that fall below overall materiality for the engagement", "Misstatements that management has agreed to correct before year-end", "Misstatements that do not involve cash or revenue accounts"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.A2, clearly trivial is not another expression for "not material." A misstatement is clearly trivial when it is inconsequential in amount, whether considered individually or in the aggregate, and by any criteria of size, nature, or circumstances. Choice B is incorrect because many misstatements fall below materiality yet are still accumulated for aggregate evaluation—only those below the clearly trivial threshold are excluded from accumulation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Timing of written communication of deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'The engagement team at Whitfield & Associates completed fieldwork for the audit of Sterling Packaging on February 15, 2026. The audit report is expected to be issued on March 10, 2026. The team identified two significant deficiencies during fieldwork. Under AU-C 265, the written communication of significant deficiencies and material weaknesses must be made:', '["Within 30 days of the audit report date regardless of circumstances", "Within 90 days after the end of the fiscal year under audit", "No later than 30 days following completion of fieldwork", "No later than 60 days following the report release date"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.12, the auditor should communicate significant deficiencies and material weaknesses in writing to those charged with governance no later than 60 days following the report release date. Best practice encourages communication before the report date when possible. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 265 specifies 60 days after the report release date—not 30 days after the report date—as the outer deadline for written communication.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L1 — Distinction between design and operation deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'What is the key distinction between a design deficiency and an operation deficiency under AU-C 265?', '["A design deficiency means a necessary control is missing or improperly structured, while an operation deficiency means a properly designed control fails to function as intended", "A design deficiency applies only to automated controls, while an operation deficiency applies only to manual controls", "A design deficiency is always a material weakness, while an operation deficiency is always a significant deficiency", "A design deficiency is identified during planning, while an operation deficiency is identified only during substantive testing"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A1, a design deficiency exists when a control necessary to meet a control objective is missing or improperly designed, meaning the objective would not be met even if the control operated as designed. An operation deficiency exists when a properly designed control fails to function effectively. Choice B is incorrect because AU-C 265 applies the design-versus-operation distinction to all controls regardless of whether they are automated or manual.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Misstatement arising from management bias in estimates
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Cascade River Industries, the auditor notices that management consistently selects accounting estimates at the optimistic end of reasonable ranges for warranty reserves, bad debt allowances, and inventory obsolescence. Each estimate is individually within an acceptable range. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should evaluate these patterns as:', '["Acceptable because each individual estimate falls within a reasonable range approved by the auditor", "Evidence that management lacks competence to prepare reliable financial statements", "Indicators of possible management bias that may cause the financial statements to be materially misstated", "Factual misstatements requiring immediate correction before the audit report is issued"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 450.A20, the auditor must evaluate whether the aggregate of uncorrected misstatements, including directional patterns in estimates, indicates bias in management''s judgments. Even when individual estimates are reasonable, a consistent pattern toward one end of the range may cause aggregate misstatement. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 450 requires the auditor to consider bias indicators even when each estimate individually falls within an acceptable range.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Communication of other (lesser) deficiencies to management
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Redstone Logistics, the engagement team identifies several control deficiencies that are individually less severe than significant deficiencies but still warrant management''s attention. Under AU-C 265, the auditor''s responsibility for communicating these other deficiencies is to:', '["Communicate them in writing to those charged with governance alongside all significant deficiencies", "Disclose them in the auditor''s report as an emphasis-of-matter paragraph noting internal control concerns", "Ignore them entirely because they do not rise to the level requiring any communication", "Communicate them to management in writing or verbally, and if verbally, document that communication in the audit file"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.14, the auditor should communicate to management other deficiencies in internal control that are not significant deficiencies or material weaknesses but are of sufficient importance to merit management''s attention. This communication may be oral or written, and if oral, the auditor should document it. Choice C is incorrect because AU-C 265 establishes a professional obligation to communicate deficiencies below the significant deficiency threshold when they merit attention.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Interim discovery of control deficiency effect on year-end procedures
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'While performing interim testing at June 30, 2025, the auditor of Copper Ridge Energy discovers that the accounts payable department has no procedure for matching purchase orders to receiving reports before processing payments. The fiscal year ends December 31, 2025. Under AU-C 265, how should this interim finding affect the auditor''s approach for the remaining audit period?', '["The auditor should treat it as resolved if management promises to implement matching procedures before year-end", "The auditor should withdraw from the engagement because the control environment is fundamentally unreliable", "The auditor should assess whether the deficiency was remediated and expand substantive testing of accounts payable for the period the deficiency existed", "The auditor should issue an interim report on the deficiency to those charged with governance immediately"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A6, when a control deficiency is identified during interim testing, the auditor must evaluate whether the deficiency persisted through year-end and adjust the nature, timing, and extent of year-end substantive procedures accordingly. Expanded testing addresses the risk during the period controls were absent. Choice A is incorrect because a management promise does not constitute remediation—the auditor must obtain evidence that corrective action was actually implemented and operating effectively.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: easy / L1 — Three categories of misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 450, what are the three categories of misstatements the auditor accumulates during an audit?', '["Factual misstatements, judgmental misstatements, and projected misstatements", "Quantitative misstatements, qualitative misstatements, and pervasive misstatements", "Known errors, likely errors, and possible errors", "Material misstatements, immaterial misstatements, and trivial misstatements"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 450.06, misstatements accumulated during the audit are classified as factual (definite errors), judgmental (differences arising from management''s estimates the auditor considers unreasonable), and projected (extrapolated from audit sample errors to the full population). Choice C is incorrect because it uses older terminology from superseded standards—AU-C 450 replaced the known/likely/possible classification with the current three-category framework.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q15: hard / L4 — Evaluate whether immaterial misstatement indicates fraud
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Pacific Rim Trading Company, the auditor discovers that the assistant controller recorded a $4,200 fictitious vendor payment and directed the funds to a personal bank account. Overall materiality is $750,000. The assistant controller has no involvement in financial reporting oversight, and the CEO and CFO were unaware of the scheme. The auditor should evaluate this situation by:', '["Treating it solely as a quantitatively immaterial factual misstatement because $4,200 is well below materiality", "Recommending that management correct the $4,200 entry and taking no further action regarding control classification", "Immediately issuing an adverse opinion because any fraud discovered during the audit requires a material weakness", "Evaluating whether the control deficiency that permitted the fraud represents a material weakness, considering the potential magnitude if the scheme had continued undetected"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.A15, the identification of fraud is a strong indicator of a material weakness, even when the actual misstatement is immaterial. The auditor must evaluate the potential magnitude—not just the actual amount detected—because the deficiency that permitted the fraud could allow larger or repeated misappropriations. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 265 requires qualitative consideration of fraud indicators beyond the dollar amount, and choice C overstates the requirement since fraud does not automatically mandate an adverse opinion.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Control deficiency impact on nature/timing/extent of procedures
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Lakewood Community Bank, the auditor determines that loan officers do not consistently document their review of borrower creditworthiness before approving loans under $100,000. This represents a significant deficiency in the lending process. Under AU-C 265, what is the most likely effect on the auditor''s planned substantive procedures?', '["No effect, because substantive procedures are independent of control testing results", "The auditor should increase the extent of loan file testing and possibly test closer to year-end rather than at an interim date", "The auditor should reduce testing because the deficiency has already been identified and formally communicated", "The auditor should test only loans exceeding $100,000 where the approval control is operating"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 265.A6, identified control deficiencies affect the auditor''s risk assessment and require adjustments to the nature, timing, and extent of substantive procedures. A significant deficiency in loan approval suggests higher risk of misstatement, warranting expanded sample sizes and testing closer to period-end. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 330 explicitly links assessed risk levels—informed by control deficiency findings—to the design of substantive procedures.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: hard / L4 — Assess competing positions on pervasiveness of misstatement
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Silverline Pharmaceuticals has revenue of $85 million. The auditor has identified uncorrected misstatements totaling $3.2 million, with overall materiality set at $2.5 million. The misstatements are concentrated in accounts receivable. Management argues that because the errors affect only one account, a qualified opinion is appropriate. Under AU-C 705, the auditor should assess:', '["Whether the misstatements are confined to specific elements and do not represent a substantial proportion of the financial statements, supporting a qualified opinion", "Whether the financial statements as a whole cannot be relied upon, requiring an adverse opinion regardless of accounts affected", "Whether management''s argument is persuasive and defer to management''s preferred opinion type", "Whether concentration in one account automatically makes the misstatement pervasive, requiring an adverse opinion"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 705.08, a qualified opinion is appropriate when misstatements are material but not pervasive—meaning they do not affect a substantial proportion of the financial statements. When errors are confined to accounts receivable and do not undermine the overall financial statement presentation, pervasiveness may be limited. Choice D is incorrect because concentration in one account does not automatically make a misstatement pervasive—pervasiveness depends on whether effects are widespread or fundamental to users'' understanding.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Qualitative vs quantitative evaluation of misstatements
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'The auditor of Thornbury Retail Group identifies an uncorrected $15,000 misclassification between selling expenses and cost of goods sold. Overall materiality is $400,000. The misstatement does not affect net income or total assets, only income statement subtotals. Under AU-C 450, the auditor should evaluate the qualitative significance by considering:', '["Only the dollar amount relative to materiality, concluding the misstatement is clearly immaterial", "Whether the misclassification affects compliance with debt covenants, regulatory thresholds, or management compensation arrangements tied to gross margin", "Whether the misstatement was intentional, and if not, disregard it from further evaluation entirely", "Only whether the misstatement affects the balance sheet, since income statement classification errors are never qualitatively material"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 450.A18, the auditor evaluates both quantitative and qualitative aspects of uncorrected misstatements. Even a quantitatively small misclassification can be qualitatively material if it affects gross margin calculations tied to loan covenants, management bonuses, or regulatory reporting. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 450 explicitly requires qualitative evaluation alongside quantitative analysis—a misstatement''s significance is not determined by its dollar amount alone.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: medium / L3 — Roll-forward considerations for interim deficiencies
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During interim testing at September 30, 2025, the auditor of Meridian Software identified a material weakness in the revenue cut-off process. By December 31, 2025, management implemented new automated cut-off controls and tested them for one month. The auditor must assess whether to report the material weakness for the year ended December 31, 2025. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should consider:', '["The deficiency is no longer reportable because management remediated it before year-end", "Only whether the new controls were in place at December 31, regardless of how long they operated effectively", "Whether sufficient time has elapsed to test the operating effectiveness of the new controls and whether the deficiency existed for a sufficient portion of the year to remain a current-year finding", "The deficiency should automatically carry forward from the interim finding without any further evaluation"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A8, the auditor evaluates both whether the remediated control has operated for a sufficient period to conclude on its effectiveness and whether the deficiency existed for a significant portion of the audit period. One month of operation may be insufficient to conclude the material weakness was remediated. Choice A is incorrect because a late-period remediation does not eliminate reporting responsibility—the deficiency existed for nine months and the new control has limited operating history.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Deficiency in operation definition
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, what characterizes a deficiency in operation of internal control?', '["A properly designed control does not operate effectively or the person performing it lacks competence", "A necessary control is missing from the organization''s control activities", "Management intentionally overrides a control for fraudulent reporting purposes", "The auditor disagrees with the design of a particular control procedure"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A1, a deficiency in operation exists when a properly designed control does not operate effectively, or when the person performing the control does not possess the necessary authority or competence. The key distinction is that the control''s design is adequate, but its execution fails. Choice B describes a deficiency in design rather than operation—the absence of a control is a design issue, not an operational one.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: easy / L1 — Restricting distribution of deficiency communication
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, the auditor''s written communication of significant deficiencies and material weaknesses is intended solely for the use of:', '["The SEC and applicable state regulators overseeing the entity", "External creditors and shareholders of the entity", "Prospective investors evaluating the entity''s financial condition", "Those charged with governance and management of the entity"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under AU-C 265.11, the written communication of significant deficiencies and material weaknesses is intended solely for the information and use of management and those charged with governance and is not suitable for any other purpose. Choice A is incorrect because the communication is not directed to regulators—while regulators may sometimes request such information, the auditor''s communication is restricted to internal governance and management.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q22: hard / L4 — Evaluate management's corrective action plan sufficiency
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Following the audit of Westbrook Financial Advisors, the auditor communicated a material weakness in the client trust account reconciliation process. Management responds with a corrective action plan proposing to hire an additional accountant and implement monthly reconciliations beginning in six months. The audit committee asks the auditor to evaluate the plan. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should:', '["Evaluate whether the planned timeline and actions are sufficient to remediate the deficiency and communicate any concerns to those charged with governance", "Endorse the plan in writing because it directly addresses the root cause of the material weakness", "Refuse to evaluate the plan because AU-C 265 does not require the auditor to verify management''s response", "Require management to implement the plan immediately as a condition of issuing the audit report"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.A19, while the auditor is not required to verify management''s corrective actions, when asked by governance to assess a remediation plan, the auditor may evaluate whether the proposed actions reasonably address the identified weakness and communicate concerns about timeline or adequacy. Choice C is too restrictive—while AU-C 265 does not mandate plan evaluation, responding to the audit committee''s specific request is appropriate professional service.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Accounting staff turnover as control environment indicator
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During the audit of Hilltop Beverage Company, the auditor learns that the accounting department experienced 80% turnover during the year, with three different controllers in 12 months. Several account reconciliations were not performed during the transition periods. Under AU-C 265, the auditor should analyze this situation by:', '["Treating the turnover as a human resources matter outside the scope of the financial statement audit", "Noting the turnover in the audit file but taking no further action because reconciliations are detective controls only", "Evaluating whether the high turnover and resulting reconciliation gaps indicate a control environment deficiency that may constitute a significant deficiency or material weakness", "Communicating the staffing issue to management orally without classifying it as a control deficiency"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.A12, the competence of personnel performing control activities is relevant to evaluating deficiency severity. High turnover disrupting reconciliation processes indicates potential control environment weaknesses that could allow misstatements to go undetected. The auditor should assess the combined effect on reporting reliability. Choice A is incorrect because personnel competence and staffing continuity directly affect internal control effectiveness and are within the scope of the auditor''s control evaluation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: easy / L2 — Tolerable misstatement and performance materiality relationship
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'The auditor of Aspen Creek Enterprises sets overall materiality at $300,000 and performance materiality at $210,000. When testing a sample from the revenue population, the auditor uses a tolerable misstatement of $210,000. Under AU-C 530, the tolerable misstatement for a sampling application:', '["Must exceed overall materiality to provide a sufficient safety margin for sampling risk", "Should not exceed performance materiality set for the financial statement line being tested", "Must equal exactly 50% of overall materiality by professional standard requirement", "Should be set at the clearly trivial threshold established for the engagement"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 530.06, tolerable misstatement is the application of performance materiality to a particular sampling procedure. It should not exceed the performance materiality amount because it represents the maximum misstatement the auditor is willing to accept in a population. Choice C is incorrect because there is no fixed percentage requirement—tolerable misstatement is based on the auditor''s judgment and relates to performance materiality, not a formulaic calculation.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — What triggers a financial statement restatement
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 560, a restatement of previously issued financial statements is required when:', '["Any misstatement, regardless of size, is found after the audit report date", "Management voluntarily decides to improve the quality of financial disclosures", "A material misstatement is discovered in previously issued financial statements", "The auditor issues a going concern opinion in the subsequent audit period"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 560.14, when the auditor becomes aware of facts that existed at the date of the auditor''s report that would have caused the auditor to amend the report, and the financial statements contain a material misstatement, the auditor should discuss the matter with management and request restatement. Choice A is incorrect because only material misstatements require restatement—immaterial errors discovered after report issuance do not trigger the restatement obligation.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Documentation requirements for deficiency evaluation
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 265, what must the auditor document regarding the evaluation of identified control deficiencies?', '["The basis for the determination of whether identified deficiencies are significant deficiencies or material weaknesses", "Only those deficiencies that are ultimately classified as material weaknesses", "A complete listing of all controls tested during the audit regardless of whether deficiencies were found", "The total number of hours spent evaluating each identified control deficiency"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under AU-C 265.16, the auditor must document the basis for determining whether identified deficiencies, individually or in combination, are significant deficiencies or material weaknesses. This documentation supports the auditor''s professional judgment in classifying each deficiency. Choice B is incorrect because AU-C 265 requires documentation of the evaluation process for all identified deficiencies considered significant enough to evaluate, not just those ultimately classified as material weaknesses.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L2 — Operating effectiveness testing reveals control deficiency
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'During operating effectiveness testing at Cedar Point Manufacturing, the auditor tests 40 sales order approvals and finds that 6 were processed without the required credit check. The expected deviation rate was 2%. Under AU-C 330, the auditor should respond to this 15% deviation rate by:', '["Accepting the results because the sample of 40 is too small to draw reliable conclusions", "Evaluating the control deficiency, increasing the assessed risk of material misstatement, and modifying the planned substantive procedures accordingly", "Retesting with a larger sample to determine whether the initial results were merely an anomaly", "Issuing a qualified opinion because the controls over revenue are not operating effectively"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 330.17, when tests of controls reveal a deviation rate exceeding expectations, the auditor should evaluate the implications for assessed risk and modify the nature, timing, or extent of planned substantive procedures. A 15% deviation rate significantly exceeds the 2% expectation, indicating the control is not operating effectively. Choice C is incorrect because AU-C 330 requires the auditor to respond to evidence obtained—not to keep retesting in hopes of better results.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q28: hard / L4 — Restrictions on deficiency communication distribution
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'After completing the audit of Harborview Medical Center, the engagement partner prepares the written communication of significant deficiencies and material weaknesses. The audit committee chairman requests that the letter be shared with the hospital''s primary lender to support a loan renewal application. The engagement partner should evaluate this request by:', '["Agreeing immediately because the audit committee has authority to distribute all audit-related communications", "Revising the letter to remove sensitive details before allowing distribution to the lending institution", "Explaining that the communication includes a restriction on use and is not intended for parties other than management and those charged with governance, and recommending the audit committee consult legal counsel", "Refusing to issue the communication entirely if there is any possibility of distribution to third parties"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under AU-C 265.11, the written communication of significant deficiencies and material weaknesses includes a restriction stating it is intended solely for management and those charged with governance. The auditor should advise the audit committee about these restrictions and the potential implications of broader distribution. Choice A is incorrect because governance authority does not override the auditor''s restricted-use designation—AU-C 265 specifically limits the intended recipients.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Effect of control deficiency on detection risk
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Under AU-C 315, a control deficiency that increases the assessed risk of material misstatement requires the auditor to:', '["Increase the acceptable level of detection risk to compensate for the higher control risk", "Decrease the acceptable level of detection risk by performing more effective substantive procedures", "Withdraw from the audit engagement when any control deficiency is identified", "Communicate with the predecessor auditor about the nature of the deficiency"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 315.27, when assessed risk of material misstatement increases due to control deficiencies, the auditor must decrease detection risk by designing more effective substantive procedures—testing larger samples, testing at year-end rather than interim, or using more precise techniques. Choice A is incorrect because increasing detection risk would provide less assurance, which is the opposite of the required response when control risk has increased.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: hard / L4 — Auditor response to subsequently discovered facts indicating misstatement
(1, 'Misstatements and Control Deficiencies', 'Three weeks after issuing the audit report for Bayshore Capital Management, the engagement partner learns from a news report that the client''s CFO had been systematically overriding revenue recognition controls throughout the audit period. The override was not detected during the audit. The engagement partner must evaluate the appropriate response. Under AU-C 560, the auditor should:', '["Take no action because the audit report has already been issued and the auditor''s responsibility has ended", "Discuss the matter with management, determine whether the financial statements need revision, and if management does not take appropriate action, notify those charged with governance and consider the effect on the auditor''s report", "Immediately withdraw the audit report without first discussing the matter with management", "Issue a new audit report with an emphasis-of-matter paragraph but make no changes to the opinion"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under AU-C 560.14–.17, when the auditor becomes aware after the report date of facts that may have affected the report, the auditor should discuss the matter with management, determine whether revision is needed, and evaluate the appropriate action regarding the previously issued report. If management refuses to act, the auditor should notify governance. Choice A is incorrect because AU-C 560 explicitly extends the auditor''s responsibilities to subsequently discovered facts—the auditor cannot ignore credible evidence of material misstatement.', 'hard', 4)
;

COMMIT;
